WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.287 --> 00:00:02.883
- [Voiceover] This is Bertha.

00:00:02.883 --> 00:00:07.333
She's a lemur, more
specifically, a coquerel’s sifaka.

00:00:07.333 --> 00:00:09.296
While all of Madagascar lemurs are rare,

00:00:09.296 --> 00:00:11.879
she's unique even for her kind.

00:00:14.202 --> 00:00:17.711
There are over 100 different
types of lemurs in Madagascar.

00:00:17.711 --> 00:00:20.730
But none quite like the coquerel’s sifaka.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:23.691
Found only in the Northwestern
Forest of Madagascar,

00:00:23.691 --> 00:00:26.302
sifakas move differently than most lemurs.

00:00:26.302 --> 00:00:28.776
They stand on two feet and
use their powerful hind legs

00:00:28.776 --> 00:00:32.099
to leap up to 30 feet in a single jump.

00:00:32.099 --> 00:00:35.104
Their beautiful fur
also distinguishes them.

00:00:35.104 --> 00:00:38.688
Their cream colored bodies
feature maroon patches.

00:00:38.688 --> 00:00:42.469
Bertha loves her peanuts but
sifakas aren't picky eaters.

00:00:42.469 --> 00:00:45.314
They consume everything
from leaves and flowers

00:00:45.314 --> 00:00:47.454
to bark and dead wood.

00:00:47.454 --> 00:00:51.104
Biologists estimate that less
than 10,000 remain in the wild

00:00:51.104 --> 00:00:55.839
as major habitat laws and
hunting continues to increase.

00:00:55.839 --> 00:00:58.172
This is the coquerel’s sifaka.

